ABSTRACT
The roughness features on the wall affect the pressure drop in the fluid. Their effect on
the flow is manifested by (i) flow area constriction, and (ii) increase in the wall shear
stress due to flow over the roughness features. Applying the area constriction to the
Moody’s chart results in a simplified plot that yields an asymptotic value of friction
factor for higher relative roughness values in the turbulent region. Specific experiments
were conducted to study the effect of surface roughness characteristics on pressure drop
using parallel saw-tooth elements in aligned and offset configurations in rectangular
channels with different channel heights. The experimental results indicate that the use of
constricted flow cross-sectional area, based on Dcf = Dt-2ε, in the modified Moody plot is
able to extend the applicability of the laminar flow friction factor equation to the relative
roughness values (defined as the saw-tooth height) of up to 14%. In the turbulent region,
there is a need to further characterize the surface features beyond the average roughness,


2
Ra, which is used extensively in the fluid mechanics and convective heat transfer
applications. A number of available roughness characteristics are reviewed for surface
characterization in minichannels and microchannels, where large relative roughness
values may be encountered.


