INTRODUCTION
The effects of surface roughness on pressure drop in circular and rectangular tubes
have been studied extensively in literature. As early as in the nineteenth century, Darcy
[1] conducted careful pressure drop experiments on pipes of different materials and
roughness, and introduced the concept of relative roughness. He also established that the
flow depended on the pipe roughness, pipe diameter, and slope. Fanning [2] later
proposed a correlation for the pressure drop as a function of surface roughness. The
pioneering work of quantifying the effect of surface roughness on pressure drop was led
by Nikuradse [3], who established the sand grain roughness, equivalent to the diameter of
the sand particles, as a major parameter in defining the friction factor during laminar and
turbulent flow studies. To create the roughness on the pipes, he first sifted sand grains so
that the grains were of uniform size. He then used Japanese lacquer to stick the sand
grains to the walls of the pipe. Through exhaustive experimentation, Nikuradse studied
the effect of roughness on flow characteristics in circular pipes. Experimental data was
obtained for water flowing in pipes of six different relative roughness surfaces with Re
ranging from 600 to 106. The dimensions of the inner diameters of the test pipes were 25,
50, and 100 mm. His work helped establish the effect of relative roughness (ε/Dt), on the
flow characteristics. It should be noted that the pipe diameter used in his work was the
base diameter of the pipe before applying the sand grains.
Colebrook [4] later published his findings on the variation in surface roughness and
its effects on pressure drop. Colebrook’s experiments included sixteen spun concrete
lined pipes and six spun bitumastic-lined pipes ranging in diameter from 4 inches to 60
inches. Average relative roughness values ranged from 0.04318 mm to 0.254 mm. Also


4
tested was the 216-inch diameter Ontario tunnel. The Ontario tunnel was a concrete
tunnel made from steel oil forms. When the concrete set and the forms were removed,
the walls of the tunnel were rubbed down with carborundum brick to remove any
deformities. These pipes were tested in the transition and fully turbulent regions. It was
determined that the friction factor decreases as the velocity increases. Once the velocity
profile reaches the fully developed profile described by the square law, the friction factor
remains constant for all higher velocities.
Moody [5] later characterized the friction factor (f) as a function of the Reynolds
number and relative roughness (ε/Dt). Through Colebrook’s work, the Moody diagram
was established which provided engineers with a convenient method of determining the
pressure drop associated with the relative roughness of the pipe. The outlined method of
determining pressure drop loses its integrity when the surface roughness is on the same
order of magnitude as the characteristic diameter of the pipe. Although the current
relative roughness values used in the plots go only as high as 5 percent, higher values are
expected while dealing with flows in small diameter channels. The sand grain diameters
in Nikuradse’s experiments were used for roughness ε. The average roughness value, Ra,
obtained from surface profile measurements were later employed by researchers and
designers while using the Moody diagram.
Webb, et al. [6] studied the separation of flow from the wall of a pipe when passed
over repeating roughness elements. Flow was determined to separate from the wall
following roughness elements and reattach at a distance of 6-8 times the height of the
element. When the roughness elements are the result of a manufacturing process such as
a cutting blade or surface treatment, the elements are similar in nature and closely spaced.


5
The flow encounters the leading element and skims over a pocket of circulating liquid
before passing over the following element.
In the present paper, the constriction effect on the Moody diagram is plotted by
considering the constricted flow area in the turbulent region. Further, to ascertain the
effect of surface roughness features on the friction factor, systematic experiments are
conducted using air and water as working fluids. The need for a new set of surface
roughness descriptors for fluid flow characterization is then discussed.
FLOW CONSTRICTION EFFECT ON FRICTION FACTOR
The Moody diagram, shown in Figure 1, is simply the Colebrook-White equation
plotted over a wide range of Reynolds numbers and relative roughness values. It serves
as an easy way for engineers to estimate the friction factor without having to perform a
calculation for the friction factor. By calculating the Reynolds numbers and relative
roughness value, an engineer can quickly find a pipes friction factor. By finding where
the calculated Reynolds number intersects the relative roughness line for a particular
roughness and tracing the intersection back to the friction factor on the y axis, the friction
factor of a given flow may be determined with minimal effort.
As seen from Fig. 1, the laminar friction factor is independent of the relative
roughness in the range ε/Dt ≤ 0.05. In the turbulent region, the friction factor for smooth
tubes continuously decreases with increasing Reynolds numbers. For rough tubes, the
friction factor follows the smooth tube plot up to a certain point, and then approaches an
asymptotic value depending on the relative roughness. The asymptotic value increases
with relative roughness as shown in Fig. 1 in the range 0 ≤ ε/Dt ≤ 0.05.


6
The above trend in friction factor in the turbulent region can be explained as follows:
For a given relative roughness, the roughness features do not affect the flow up to a
certain value of Reynolds number. For higher Reynolds number values, the surface
effects are felt in the flow, eventually reaching an asymptotic friction factor value.
The calculations of Moody assumed that the flow boundary occurred at the wall of
the tube and utilized the full diameter for flow area. Following the discussion presented
by Webb et al. [6], Fig. 2 is drawn to show the effects of roughness elements on the
reattachment of flow to the pipe walls for different roughness heights. It can be seen
from Fig. 2 that for low roughness values, the flow reattaches to the tube wall. At higher
roughness values, the flow does not re-attach to the pipe wall, thus decreasing the
available pipe area for fluid flow. The new flow boundary is suspended above the
surface wall at a distance y. The diameter of the flow, then, is not the diameter of the
pipe, but is reduced by the size of the roughness elements. While the exact value of this
height in comparison to the height of the roughness elements is not known, it is bounded
by y=0 and the height of the elements. In closely spaced roughnesses, such as the sand
grains used in Nikuradse’s experiments, the value of y closely resembles ε. The new
flow diameter can then be approximated by:
