Figure 2 shows the Modified Moody diagram plotted using eqs. (5) and (6) in the
turbulent region with friction factor fcf and Reynolds number Recf based on Dcf . It can be
seen from Fig. 2 that the essential features of the Moody diagram are retained, in terms of
the transition region leading to an asymptotic value of f at higher Reynolds number.
However, a major change is seen in that the asymptotic value of fcf, obtained for ε/Dt
>0.03 at higher Recf values, plateaus to approximately fcf=0.042. In other words, for
relative roughness values higher than 0.03, the friction factor fcf based on the constricted
diameter Dcf is constant in the fully rough region. This means that the roughness effects
on the friction factor are completely accounted for through the area constriction effects
incorporated in fcf. Intuitively, this makes sense as a higher roughness feature would
result in a higher flow constriction. This corresponds to the region described by Webb et
al. [6] where the flow does not reattach to the walls as shown in Fig. 2.
It is strongly advised that the above plots are valid only in the range 0 ≤ ε/Dt ≤ 0.05
as in the original Moody diagram. There is a need for reliable experimental data in the
region ε/Dt > 0.05 for circular pipes before the Moody diagram can be extended to pipes
with higher relative roughnesses. The effect of the constriction in the laminar region is
not as pronounced, especially for ε/Dt ≤ 0.05. For higher values of relative roughness,
experimental confirmation is needed in both laminar and turbulent ranges.
In using the Moody diagram, one would measure the tube inner diameter, which in
reality represents Dcf and use it as Dt in pressure drop calculations. In Nikuradse’s
experiments, the base diameter Dt was employed in the pressure drop calculations.


