Although this error is quite small for the range of roughness employed in the Moody
diagram, it could become significant if the diameter correction is not incorporated while
extending the Moody diagram for pipes with higher roughness values. The fact that fcf
remains constant at 0.042 in the high Reynolds number region also simplifies the
calculations to some extent. It should be noted that in calculating the pressure drop, the
actual constricted flow area should be used as given by eq. (5).
ROUGHNESS EFFECT ON FRICTION FACTOR
The roughness imparted on the internal walls of a flow channel are dependent on the
manufacturing processes. The sand grain roughness feature is used extensively in fluid
mechanics application. With smaller channel sizes, the relative roughness could be
significantly greater than the threshold of 0.05 used in the Moody diagram. A survey of
the manufacturing processes is presented first, followed by the relevant roughness
parameters that hold promise. Finally, the effect of high relative roughness and one
specific roughness feature on the friction factor in the laminar and transition region is
investigated.
SURVEY OF MANUFACTURING PROCESSES
Process develops surface geometry and roughness
All manufactured products are created with specific materials and geometry to
accomplish the function for which they were intended. The specific geometry of parts
can be created via any of the major subsets of processes; phase change processes,


10
deformation processes, material addition processes, or material removal processes. All
processes have a direct effect on material properties and vice versa. The most typical
scenario in manufacturing is a type of balancing act in selecting materials and processes
that come together with a proper design to generate the desired resulting product.
Phase-change processes
Casting and molding processes generate part geometry via a phase-change from liquid to
solid. The process is extremely efficient and is typically adapted to high volume
production processes. The most typical drawback to casting and molding processes is a
lack of dimensional control and poor material properties. The thermal contraction of
materials and the volume contraction associated with the liquid to solid phase change
causes severe difficulties when working at a mini or micro scale. The difficulty of
holding any dimensional tolerances with complex geometries is usually prohibitive at a
very small scale. If dimensional properties can be maintained, the poor material strength
associated with casting and molding due to the properties and the direction of grain
growth is typically prohibitive.
At the small scale there are also some deposition and plating processes that make use of
vapor or liquid to solid transformations, typically for coating applications. These
processes are typically included in material addition processes and will be discussed
further in that section below. Complex geometry and minute features are not possible for
mechanical structures in vapor deposition processes, but masking and etching


11
technologies do make such processes candidates for simple structures and geometry
generation.
Deformation processes
Macro scale processes such as forging, punching, and rolling are typically not utilized at
the mini or micro scales, though some examples do exist. This class of processes makes
use of plastic deformation to change the size and shape of materials until they meet
design requirements. The materials experience severe strains and typically undergo strain
hardening that can drastically change material properties. At the small scale these are
often prohibitive characteristics, but some examples of successful processes do exist.
Micro wire drawing processes are routinely used to make very fine metal wire for
external connectivity and other applications.
Material addition processes
Electronics processing and rapid prototyping technologies have made use of
manufacturing processes that actually add material to the part rather than removing it to
generate the desired geometry. This style of process has the advantage that material is
only deposited where it is needed and is therefore very efficient from a time and energy
usage perspective. The main drawback of such processes is the poor material properties
of the material that is deposited due to interfacial bonding issues. Control of bonding to
the initial substrate and crystal structure growth are typically very poor in material
addition processes and the result is very poor mechanical performance of the parts and
somewhat compromised electrical properties. Such processes are often necessary


12
because there is no other way to accomplish the required design, but are typically avoided
in most situations.
Material removal processes
Machining is the most developed of all processes and very controlled dimensions and
material properties can be achieved with most materials in use today. Many different
methods for material removal have been developed including rotating, abrasive, laser,
water jet, acid etch, electrical discharge, and other removal processes. All material
removal processes work as a type of sculpting process, methodically removing material
until the desired part geometry is obtained from a larger block of material. All material
removal processes generate the gross geometry of the part with relative success, but the
fine features of the surface that is generated vary a great deal depending on the material
removal method used. As the feature sizes of the parts are reduced the relative
importance of the surface features is amplified, creating not only a texture, but actual
features of a size that is relevant on the part surface.
Traditional machining
Traditional material removal processes make use of the rotation of a spindle to generate
relative motion between the work part and the tool. In the case of a cylindrical
component, the part is rotated in the spindle and the tool is pseudo stationary as it
generates the desired part profile. In the case of prismatic components, the part is held
stationary while the tool is rotated in the spindle and forced to interfere with the part in
such as way as to generate the desired part geometry. The nature of the material removal


13
process is such that the tools will remove material by following paths that overlap to
some degree (but not completely) to generate the gross geometry of the part and as a
byproduct the fine features of the surface. For the purposes of this paper, it is assumed
that the gross geometry of the parts is attainable without difficulty, but it is the relative
size of the fine surface features (tool marks) with respect to the devices being fabricated
that are of most concern because they will in fact have drastic impacts upon the
performance of the devices. With traditional spindle-based machining processes fine tool
marks occur at regular intervals (typically related to the feed rate of the tool) and have a
geometry that is the inverse of the tool tip. A typical surface generated in a turning
process is shown in Figure 3. In the mini / micro range the relative size and impact of
these surface features is increased dramatically.
Nontraditional material removal processes
The past few decades have seen the development of a host of material removal processes
that do not make use of a rotating spindle and tool in the traditional manner. Processes
that fall under this category include laser machining, water jet cutting, plasma arc cutting,
ultrasonic machining, etc. One of the most common of these processes is electrical
discharge machining (EDM) in which a surface is eroded via electrical arcing from a tool
to the part. As the process progresses the gross geometry of the part takes on the inverse
shape of the tool that is used to generate it. The process is commonly used for complex
die cavity generation because the process can use relatively soft and easily machinable
graphite or copper electrodes to machine extremely tough tool steel parts with very
complex geometry. The fine surface features of this process, as well as most


14
nontraditional processes are more random in frequency, size, and shape. Figure 4 shows
the surface features of a part that was created using EDM.
Etching processes
Another set of material removal processes relies on chemical means to remove material
and have become of critical importance in electronic manufacturing. Acid/chemical
etching and electrically assisted etching techniques are effective in removing material,
but great care must be taken to generate the desired geometry by masking or other means.
Etching processes are particularly sensitive to crystal structures and grain boundaries that
cause variations in the etching rates at different places on the same part. In general
etched surfaces take on the features and character of the underlying material structure.
These features are typically magnitude limited only by the time and extent of the etching
process.
Micro fabrication processes
The advent of electronics and their ever-decreasing feature sizes has had a strong impact
on the development of processes to support such fabrication. This set of processes has
become known as micro fabrication technology, but is actually made up of a series of
different processes. Layered integrated circuits have caused the development of both
material removal and material addition processes in addition to doping processes which
change the electrical properties of the parts. The gross and fine geometry produced by
the removal and addition processes is typically layered in nature, but does consist of full
3-D features. Figure 5 shows the gross geometry of a silicon substrate after processing,


15
and the chips on the surface. The fine features of the micro-fabricated parts are generated
by a series of masking, etching, and deposition processes that perform according to the
descriptions above. The general character of the features can often be exaggerated due to
the large number of sequential steps involved in the production of layered IC chips and
other micro fabricated parts. Figure 6 shows the typical dimensional range of micro
fabrication processes as compared to conventional machining processes.
Surfaces
The assessment of surfaces is a difficult and confusing proposition for most situations in
manufacturing and production environments. The link between design intent and the
performance of a given surface is usually tenuous at best and is most often based on
experience and feedback from field experience, which is clearly post-manufacturing. In
general manufacturing and production strive to provide what the designer has called out
whether the designer had a good reason to call it out or not. A big part of this process is
the measurement and characterization of surfaces. Most typically a surface is
characterized by gross, medium, and fine features separately. Gross features are typically
characterized by dimensions and tolerances. Medium features typically are characterized
as large wavelength surface variations known as waviness or flatness. Fine surface
features are typically described as high frequency variations in the surface, better known
as roughness. Figure 7 shows the difference between medium and fine surface features.
Characterization of surfaces is typically done on a worst case scenario basis, measuring
variations across the lay of the part, the lay typically being tool marks.


