EFFECT OF ROUGHNESS FEATURES ON FRICTION FACTOR
The Moody diagram is applicable to pipes that have relatively low values of relative
surface roughness. The underlying data in this diagram is based on sand grain roughness.
This diagram is used extensively in predicting friction factors for commercial tubes
whose roughness features are not too different from the sand grain roughness. As the
relative roughness becomes large, it becomes important to consider the roughness
features as well.
Need for roughness characterization
The pioneering work on characterizing the effect of surface roughness on pressure drop
by Nikuradse [1] was achieved by using sand to induce known roughnesses on flow
pipes. This work provided the foundations for establishing the effects of relative
roughness (e/D) on flow characteristics. However, this characterization of surface
roughness was limited to the diameter of the sand grain (equivalent to the modern
descriptor Average Maximum Height of the Profile, Rz) and did not consider other
parameters that would describe the spacing, waviness, and shape in a three-dimensional
area profiling. Additionally, using the definitions by Kandlikar and Grande [7], when
considering minichannels (3 mm ≥ Dh ≥ 200 μm) and microchannels (200 μm ≥ Dh ≥ 10
μm), the ratio between the hydraulic diameter and the geometry of the roughness profile
becomes significantly different from those in conventional channels. At this scale, the
shape, spacing and size of the roughness irregularities have different influence upon the
pressure drop and the overall fluid regime. In particular, a more complete characterization
may prove useful in attempting to describe the flow separation behavior exhibited in flow


17
patterns when encountering features from the surface topography. Hence, there is a need
for developing a set of roughness descriptors suitable for micro and mini channels that
would allow for a complete understanding the effect of different aspects of surface
topography upon pressure drop and fluid behavior.
Surface characterization parameters and possibilities
In any given channel cross section there are two surfaces with roughness features of
interest (see Figure 8). The proposed approach formalizes a set of descriptors to be
collected from each of the two surfaces, as well as statistics on the hydraulic diameter
from the cross section of interest describing the interaction between the two surfaces.
