EXPERIMENTAL VERIFICATION OF THE ROUGHNESS AND FEATURE
EFFECTS
The effect of roughness beyond ε /Dt > 0.05 is not studied in literature. The higher
roughness is usually incorporated by incorporating surface features. The effect of feature
size, shape and distribution on friction factor then becomes important. To verify this
effect, Schmitt [9] performed an experimental study to evaluate the friction factor for a
grooved surface in a rectangular minichannel. Two cases were investigated with the
grooves in the top and bottom channel surfaces either aligned or offset in relation to each
other. The details of the experimental setup and the test sections are given by Schmitt [9],
and some essential features are described below.
Both air and water have been used as test fluids in the present work. Figures 16 and
17 show the respective experimental loops.
For the experiments with air, zero air is supplied in pressurized cylinders. The air
is then passed through a bank of flow meters before entering the test section as shown in
Fig. 16. The test section is 80 mm long and has thirteen pressure taps located along its
length. For experiments with water, deionized (DI), degassed water is used as the test


20
fluid. The water is degassed using the procedure described in Kandlikar et al [10]. The
DI, degassed water is drained into a Fisher Scientific Isotemp 3013 water bath. A
positive displacement 1/4 HP bronze gear pump capable of pumping up to 0.9 GPM feeds
the test section. A by-pass loop is used to control the flow rate through the test section.
The water is then filtered using a Shelco FOS housing with a 1μm wound fiber filter.
Three Omega FL-5500-NV rotameter flow meters control the flow rate to the test section.
The pressure taps from the test section are connected to a manifold through valves
to facilitate differential pressure measurement, measured from the manifold inlet. Four
pressure transducers with ±1psi, ±5psi, ±15psi, or ±30psi differential pressure ranges
respectively are used to cover the entire operating range with air and water. K-type
thermocouples measure the fluid temperature before and after the test section. Once the
water passes through the test section, it is recirculated into the constant temperature water
bath, while air is discharged into the atmosphere.
TEST SECTION DETAILS
Test section is made of two individual inserts, each 10 mm wide and 100 mm long,
forming the top and bottom sides of the flow channel. They are mounted in a fixture that
allows the gap between them to be controlled using two micrometers. A photograph of
the assembly is shown in Fig. 18. The fluid inlet is through smooth entrances machined
on the inserts. The surfaces of the inserts are machined to provide smooth or rough
surfaces.
Three surfaces are tested in the current study. These surfaces are (i) smooth, (ii)
aligned saw-tooth roughness, and (iii) offset saw-tooth roughness. The smooth channel is


21
shown in Figure 19. The channel is relatively smooth with a maximum average
roughness height, Rz of 1.22μm. The smooth minichannel is used to validate the test
section since a smooth channel will not deviate from the conventional flow theory.
The aligned saw-tooth roughness arrangement is shown in Figure 20. It consists of a
rectangular minichannel having parallel surfaces with machined ribs running
perpendicular to the flow direction. The surface roughness peaks on one side of the
channel are lined up to match the surface roughness peaks on the opposite side of the
channel.
The offset saw-tooth roughness arrangement also consists of parallel surfaces with
machined ribs running perpendicular to the flow direction, but the surface roughness
peaks are lined up such that the point of one surface roughness peak on one side lies
centered between the two surface roughness peaks directly across from it. This is shown
in Figure 21.
The surface roughness parameters were measured using a Mitotoyo SJ-400
profilometer. The results are shown below.
