EFFECT OF ROUGHNESS STRUCTURE
The experiments were conducted with two configurations with grooved inserts as
shown in Figs. 20 and 21. The gap between the inserts was systematically varied to yield
different ε/Dcf ratios. The hydraulic diameter was calculated using the constricted flow
area, with width as w and the constricted gap bcf = b-2ε (see Figs. 20 and 21).
Figures 24 - 27 show the friction factor variation with Recf for the two configurations
– saw-tooth aligned and saw-tooth offset. For comparison, the theoretical friction factor
values are also plotted. We are interested in seeing the effect of roughness as well as the
roughness orientation on fcf.
Figures 24 and 25 show the variation of friction factor for the two cases with air and
water flow. It is seen that the data does not agree with the theoretical laminar and
turbulent cases and lie significantly above the predicted friction factors. It should be
noted that the theoretical friction factors are based on the maximum flow area based on
the root height, b.
Figures 26 and 27 show the same data presented in Figs. 24 and 25 for air and water,
but the theoretical friction factors are calculated with the constricted flow area based on
bcf. It is seen that the agreement between the experimental data and the respective
laminar flow equation is significantly improved, (to within less than 5 percent before
transition appears to set in). The modified Moody diagram is thus seen to be applicable
with the constricted flow area for both saw-tooth and aligned roughness features.
In the turbulent region, the agreement is quite poor. The nature of the roughness
elements also makes a difference. The flow does interact with the walls even at the high
relative roughness values. Using the average roughness Ra = 17 μm, the relative


25
roughness value is calculated as 17/953 = 0.018. Although this is less than 0.05, the
conventional Moody plot is not applicable. It clearly points out the need to introduce
new parameters. One of the parameters is the peak height of the element, b. Setting ε=b
is another simple way to represent the roughness elements and is used in Figs. 24 and 25.
However, the aligned and offset geometries are not represented by this parameter.
Additional parameters need to be looked at as presented in Figs 9-15. A surface profile
for the channel is shown in Figs. 2 and 21 along with some of the parameters listed at the
top of the chart. A large set of data with different surface features is needed to develop
more specific set of parameters suitable for characterization of roughness effects. Similar
results are obtained for different channel heights.
CONCLUSIONS
1. The constricted flow area has been introduced as an important parameter in
representing the effect of roughness on friction factor. The Moody diagram re
plotted with this parameter results in a constant friction factor in the fully
developed turbulent flow region.
2. The effect of surface roughness features is studied with aligned and offset saw
tooth surfaces in a rectangular geometry. The height of the saw-tooth was used to
represent the roughness ε. The height of the channel was systematically varied to
obtain friction data for air and water for ε/Dh of up to 14%.
3. In the laminar region, the use of the constricted flow area resulted in a
significantly improved agreement (within 5 percent, prior to somewhat early


26
transition to turbulent flow) with the laminar flow data for air as well as water in
both aligned and offset configurations.
4. The laminar-to-turbulent transition is not affected appreciably as the channel
hydraulic diameter decreases for channels with relative roughnesses below 0.05.
However, the transition occurs at lower Reynolds numbers as the relative
roughness increases, especially above 0.05.
5. In the turbulent region, the experimental data was considerably above the values
obtained using the constricted flow area. The effect of surface features is thus
seen to influence the friction factor. Additional data is needed to further quantify
this effect.
6. Six parameters are introduced as possible candidates in representing the
roughness effect on friction factor in the rough region with ε/D >0. Further
experimental data and analysis is needed before recommending any specific set to
represent the flow effects of surface features.


